I do not know the program, but many games can experience different problems
in the middle of a game, if you do not have enough conventional memory, i.e.
you 
should have as much memory free below 640K before you start the game. Check
with the
DOS mem command. Typically, most games today require 580-600 Kbyte free
conventional
memory.

Another problem, which I have seen, originates in the use of SCSI controllers,
that use
DMA for disk access. Check if you pc is equipped with SCSI disks. The problem
comes from
a conflict between the DMA requests from the sound board and disk accesses. No
driver updates
will help on this problem, except for loading the SCSI disk driver, that came
with your SCSI
board before the soundblaster drivers.

Hope this will help. A good place to check for driver updates and Q&A's is on
CompuServe.
Both Maxis and Creative have forums there.

